## 10.4 Competing Approaches

### C

| Concept | C | Zig |
|---------|---|-----|
| Array of fixed size | `int arr[10];` | `var arr: [10]i32 = undefined;` |
| Pass array to function | Decays to `int*`, length lost | Pass as `[]i32` (slice) or `*[10]i32` |
| Pointer to many elements | `int *p;` (no length info) | `[*]i32` or better `[]i32` |
| Bounds checking | None (unless programmer adds) | Optional (safety modes) |
| `sizeof` on array | Works (compile‑time) | `@sizeOf([N]T)` works |

C’s decay is the primary source of unsafety. Zig’s explicit slice type eliminates the decay and makes length passing mandatory. Zig also replaces C’s `void*` with typed pointers and optional nullability.

### Rust

Rust’s slices `&[T]` are very similar to Zig’s `[]T` – both are fat pointers (ptr+len). Key differences:
- Rust’s slices are always immutable unless written `&mut [T]`. Zig’s `[]T` is mutable by default; `[]const T` is the read‑only version.
- Rust has a borrow checker enforcing that references do not outlive their data. Zig has no borrow checker; the programmer relies on conventions and `defer`/`errdefer` for lifetime management.
- Rust’s `Vec<T>` owns its data and can grow; Zig’s `std.ArrayList(T)` serves the same role but requires an explicit allocator.

Zig’s philosophy: lifetime correctness is the programmer’s job, but the language gives you the tools (safety modes, explicit allocators) to avoid mistakes without the complexity of a region‑based borrow checker.

### Go

Go slices have three fields: pointer, length, **and capacity**. They support append with potential reallocation. This convenience hides allocations and can lead to surprising sharing behavior (two slices sharing the same underlying array but with different capacities). Zig’s slices lack capacity because resizing is not a slice operation – you must use `std.ArrayList` or similar, making allocations visible.

### C++

C++ offers `std::array<T, N>` (fixed size, value semantics), `std::vector<T>` (owning, dynamic), and `std::span<T>` (non‑owning view). Zig’s `[N]T` corresponds to `std::array`, `[]T` to `std::span`, and `std.ArrayList(T)` to `std::vector`. The difference: Zig’s span (`[]T`) is a first‑class language type with built‑in syntax and bounds checking in safety modes, whereas `std::span` is a library feature added in C++20. Zig avoids the template metaprogramming needed to make `std::span` generic.

