## 10.7 Best Practices

### 1. Prefer `[]T` for Function Parameters

When you need a read‑only or mutable view into a sequence, take a slice. It works with arrays, `ArrayList`, and any other contiguous memory.

```zig
fn sum(values: []const i32) i32 {
    var total: i32 = 0;
    for (values) |v| total += v;
    return total;
}
```

### 2. Use `[]const T` for Immutable Access

If you don’t need to modify the elements, write `[]const T` instead of `[]T`. This allows the caller to pass both mutable and immutable slices and documents your intent.

### 3. Avoid `[*]T` Unless Doing C Interop

Raw many‑item pointers lack length information and bounds checking. For internal Zig code, use slices. For C callbacks that demand `[*]T`, wrap them in a safe slice as soon as possible.

### 4. Return Slices from Functions When Borrowing

If a function returns a view into memory it does not own (e.g., a slice of a struct field), document the lifetime conventions. Zig has no borrow checker, so convention and careful code review must suffice.

### 5. Use `&array` to Get a Full Slice

`&array` where `array: [N]T` produces `[]T`. This is cleaner than `array[0..]`.

### 6. Compile‑Time Array Bounds Validation

For arrays with known bounds, use comptime to validate indices:

```zig
fn getAt(comptime idx: usize, arr: anytype) @TypeOf(arr)[idx] {
    if (idx >= arr.len) @compileError("Index out of bounds");
    return arr[idx];
}
```

### 7. Prefer Iterators Over Manual Indexing

```zig
for (slice, 0..) |item, i| { // only when you need the index
    // ...
}
```

